##############################################################################
# instantcoffee — single source of truth for the whole stack.
#
# This file is COMMITTED on purpose: the pins below are what make the setup
# reproducible. It contains no secrets.
#
# For machine-local overrides that should NOT be committed, create .env.local
# (gitignored) with the same keys — the scripts merge it on top of this file.
##############################################################################

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pins — bumped by scripts/update.sh, which also rewrites versions.lock.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Immutable per-build tag of ggml-org/llama.cpp (NOT the floating :server-cuda).
#
# b10200 -> b10573 (2026-08-22). The old pin sat at 2026-07-30 and the comment
# justifying it said nothing between it and the newest image was Qwen3.8-
# specific. That is no longer true; four commits in the gap bear on this stack:
#
#   2b562109 (#26079, 2026-08-20)  CUDA: per-HW/per-quant MMVQ->MMQ decode
#       crossover. b10200's ggml_cuda_should_use_mmvq() has NO Ada Lovelace
#       entry at all, so a 4090 uses the MMVQ (GEMV) kernel for every batch up
#       to MMVQ_MAX_BATCH_SIZE=8. b10573 adds a table explicitly "tuned on RTX
#       4090": Q4_K/Q5_K cross to MMQ at ne11 > 7, Q3_K at > 6, Q2_K at > 4.
#
#       BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THIS BUYS AT THE CURRENT SETTINGS: nothing.
#       The verify batch is 1+n_max, so n-max 4 gives 5, and for Q4_K both
#       builds use MMVQ at 5 (b10200 because 5 <= 8, b10573 because 5 <= 7).
#       The two builds differ only at a verify batch of exactly 8 — i.e.
#       n-max 7 — where b10200 stays on MMVQ and b10573 switches to MMQ. When
#       ngram-simple fires it drafts far past 8 (draft/cycle 26.8 on the
#       repetition bench) and both builds are on MMQ there too.
#
#       So this commit is not a free win today; it is what makes the n-max 6/8
#       rows of the sweep worth running, and it is the reason those rows would
#       have measured the wrong thing on b10200. The immediate value of the
#       bump is the three correctness fixes below.
#   26793   chat: tighten bare function parsing for Qwen models. This stack's
#       whole tool path is --jinja native function calling on a Qwen model.
#   26605   fit: fix memory allocation for MTP layers.
#   27400   common: fix draft-mtp with embeddings.
#
# Availability checked against the registry: b10549/b10581 have no published
# server-cuda image; b10573 does (sha256:f74f5805191a8030bff26e2a7890d737fc2b5
# d0931220c953d25e1b7d7613ef5). The published tags near the head are b10499,
# b10524, b10548, b10573.
#
# NOT taken: DFlash2 (PR #27342). It is still open, and a report on the PR from
# an RTX 4090 running UD-IQ4_XS against multi-turn tool-result histories - this
# stack's exact workload - measures generation collapsing 3-5x as output
# accumulates (60-105 -> 19-20 tok/s by ~28k output; 12-14 tok/s from ~200
# output tokens on every turn of a real ~200-message agentic session). draft-mtp
# and no-spec are immune on the identical request. Revisit after it merges.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# b10573 -> b10689 (2026-08-30). DFlash2 has now merged, it WAS revisited, and
# the answer is still no - see the DRAFT_MODEL_REPO block further down for the
# source-verified reason (the draft-KV hole on prefix reuse is warn-only in the
# merged code, and this stack's prompt caching + agent tool histories are the
# exact trigger). Nothing below turns it on. This bump is taken for two other
# things in the 117-commit gap:
#
#   0cc5b149 (#27679, 2026-08-25)  chat: scope the qwen3-coder workarounds.
#       THE REASON FOR THIS BUMP. common_chat_params_init_qwen3_coder was
#       pushing one `<function=NAME>` grammar TRIGGER per tool onto every
#       request, unconditionally, and a trigger is checked against every
#       sampled token. The fix gates that on `is_qwen3_coder = !supports_
#       reasoning`. This stack runs thinking mode, so supports_reasoning is
#       true and every one of those per-tool triggers now disappears.
#       It fixes #27675, which is this model and these flags (Qwen3.8-27B,
#       draft-mtp, native tools) measuring decode at 30 tok/s with 263 tools
#       against 70 without.
#
#       THAT IT APPLIES TO THIS STACK IS VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED. The dispatch
#       into that function is on template CONTENT (common/chat.cpp @ b10689:
#       `<tool_call>` AND `<function=` AND `<parameter=` all present), and
#       supports_reasoning is `tmpl.source().find("<think>") != npos`. Probed
#       off the Hub with scripts/gguf_probe.py's parser on 2026-08-30: BOTH
#       models carry all four markers - orcarouter Q4_K_M (8952-char template,
#       the uc-coding regime) and unsloth UD-Q4_K_XL (9993-char, coding). So
#       both route to qwen3_coder with supports_reasoning true, which is
#       exactly the is_qwen3_coder=false branch the fix adds.
#
#       SIZE IT HONESTLY BEFORE BELIEVING IT: #27675's 2x came from 263 tools.
#       Adapter mode puts ~10-15 schemas on the wire (docs/context-budget.md),
#       so the win here is proportionally smaller. Measure, do not assume.
#
#   2c6b141e (#27400, 2026-08-22)  common: fix draft-mtp with embeddings. The
#       MTP context was inheriting embedding/pooling settings from the main
#       context. Direct correctness fix on the spec path this stack runs.
#
# Availability checked against the registry the same way as last time, with
# b10573 as the CONTROL so a 404 means absent rather than unauthorised: of the
# recent builds only b10689 has a published server-cuda image (b10690 and
# b10678-b10688 all 404; the earlier published tags are b10680, b10666, b10644,
# b10630, b10615, b10603, b10588). b10689 =
# sha256:e52c610406cd18714902d1ca3bffadebca4a2a8370faaba8a5be5cc5d5203921, and
# all four commits above are confirmed ancestors of it.
#
# RETRACTED, so it is not a reason to hold back: a ~13% MTP decode regression
# reported inside the DFlash2 window (cobra91, RTX 5080, NVFP4) was withdrawn
# by its own reporter on 2026-08-27 after a warmup-discarded greedy re-run
# (188 pre-window vs 187 at head). Cold-start artefact, ~10% on the first
# generation after load. Worth remembering for our own benches.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS BOX, 2026-08-30. Smoke test 11/11 through forge at n_ctx
# 98304 including a real tool call, and an A/B of the grammar cost against
# b10573 on identical weights/context/config with a new probe
# (scripts/bench_tools.py; records in context/bench/tools/, block `tool_grammar`
# in versions.lock). Depth-matched cost of carrying the tool grammar:
#     tools    b10573    b10689
#     15        89.6 %    95.6 %
#     100       77.9 %    96.0 %
# b10573 degrades with tool count; b10689 is flat at ~4 %. The mechanism is
# confirmed. BUT at adapter mode's ~10-15 schemas the practical gain is ~3 %,
# which is INSIDE this box's ~6 % decode noise -- do not quote it as a speedup.
# The bump is taken for correctness and headroom. The 22 % cost at 100 tools is
# a reason never to let a client load all 98 browser schemas directly.
LLAMA_TAG=server-cuda-b10689

# The llama IMAGE, when it is not an upstream ggml-org build. Empty = upstream,
# i.e. exactly the image every earlier run of this stack used, assembled from
# LLAMA_TAG above. Nothing changes unless this is set.
#
# It exists because LLAMA_TAG names a TAG inside one fixed repository, and a
# fork lives in a different one. Three places built that repository name into a
# string literal (docker-compose.yml, capacity-probe.sh's idle-floor read,
# kld-run.sh's runner image); a probe that set LLAMA_TAG to a fork tag would
# have produced "manifest unknown" in compose and a SILENTLY WRONG idle floor in
# the probe, because gpu_mem_idle() would have failed to pull and returned an
# empty reading rather than an error.
#
# Set it to the FULL reference including the tag, e.g.
#   LLAMA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/anbeeld/beellama.cpp:server-cuda-v0.4.4
# LLAMA_TAG is then ignored for the image but is still what versions.lock and
# update.sh reason about, so DO NOT leave this set outside a probe without
# recording the fork and its base commit next to the pin.
LLAMA_IMAGE=
# forge-guardrails release from PyPI.
#
# 0.9.0 -> 0.9.5 on 2026-08-30, through update.sh, gated on the smoke test
# (11/11) and with test_forge_patches.py passing 70/70 inside the live image.
# All nine patchers in patches/ apply unchanged; upstream 0.9.5 still carries
# the bug behind forge issues #142/#151, so forge_merge_consecutive is still
# load-bearing.
#
# IF YOU EVER PIN BELOW 0.9.3, THE ENTRYPOINT MATTERS. 0.9.3 removed the
# `forge-proxy` console script from the PyPI package so the standalone installer
# could own that name; Dockerfile.forge therefore runs `python -m forge.proxy`,
# which works on every version this repo has used. With the old `forge-proxy`
# entrypoint, 0.9.3+ dies at container init with
#     exec: "forge-proxy": executable file not found in $PATH
# and that is not a Python error, so there is no traceback and no forge log
# line — it reads as a broken image rather than a renamed command.
FORGE_VERSION=0.9.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where GGUFs live on disk. Docker Desktop bind mounts the WINDOWS path, so this
# must be a //<drive>/... style path, not a WSL path. D: has the free space;
# C: does not (see context/design/decisions.md).
MODELS_DIR=//d/llm-models

# MTP is baked into the mainline 3.8 quants — there is no separate *-MTP-GGUF
# repo for 3.8 and none is needed. Verified by reading the GGUF header off the
# Hub before downloading: qwen35.nextn_predict_layers=1, block_count=65, and
# blk.64.nextn.{eh_proj,enorm,hnorm,shared_head_norm}. general.architecture is
# still `qwen35`, so the pinned llama.cpp build loads 3.8 unchanged.

# The two lines below are rewritten by scripts/mode.sh. Do not describe the
# current regime in a comment here, and do not hand-edit them to switch — edit
# modes/*.env and run the script. `./scripts/mode.sh` reports the live answer;
# modes/*.env holds the per-regime values and the full reasoning for each.
#
# What each regime points them at:
#
#   coding  unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF  ::  Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf
#           https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF
#           Unsloth Dynamic V3, stock weights, no decensoring. The committed
#           eval scorecard is measured on THIS file; keep it that way.
#
#   prose   mradermacher/Qwen3.8-27B-OBLITERATED-i1-GGUF
#           ::  Qwen3.8-27B-OBLITERATED.i1-Q4_K_M.gguf
#           https://huggingface.co/OBLITERATUS/Qwen3.8-27B-OBLITERATED
#           https://huggingface.co/mradermacher/Qwen3.8-27B-OBLITERATED-i1-GGUF
#           The imatrix (i1) requant. The source repo's OWN Q4_K_M was tried on
#           2026-08-26 and rejected — it ships a stripped chat template that
#           breaks tool calling. Full autopsy in modes/prose.env.
#           Decensored including soft "safety lecture" deflections, not just
#           hard refusals. Costs -2.1pp MMLU, concentrated in STEM (-3.3pp),
#           lightest in humanities (-1.0pp) — the right trade for fiction, and
#           exactly why it must NOT be promoted to `coding`.
#
# ALTERNATIVE decensored build, deliberately NOT selected but kept documented
# because it has the strongest published evidence of the field, and is the one
# to use if a single model ever has to serve both regimes:
#
#   JonathanColetti/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-GGUF
#   ::  Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-IQ4_XS.gguf
#   https://huggingface.co/JonathanColetti/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-GGUF
#   https://huggingface.co/JonathanColetti/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored  (bf16 source)
#   Heretic abliteration merged at bf16, so not a quantized round trip. Refusals
#   98/100 base -> 12/100 at KL 0.1191, picked off a published 23-point Pareto
#   front. Capability delta MMLU -0.2 / mean -0.5, every delta inside stderr. It
#   keeps more capability than OBLITERATUS but still refuses 12% of the time,
#   which is why it lost the prose slot. Full reasoning: modes/prose.env.
#
# Check any replacement for the MTP head by reading the GGUF header off the Hub
# BEFORE downloading: 866 tensors, block_count=65, nextn_predict_layers=1, and
# blk.64.nextn.* present. Abliteration re-saves through transformers, which
# silently drops the MTP head while config.json still advertises it — and
# --spec-type draft-mtp then dies on a file that looks correct.
MODEL_REPO=orcarouter/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-GGUF
GGUF_FILE=Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-Q4_K_M.gguf
# Name the API reports. llama.cpp ignores the model field clients send, so this
# is cosmetic — but it is what shows up in /v1/models and in client UIs.
MODEL_ALIAS=qwen3.8-27b

# Vision projector. Empty = text-only (the default; forge does not use vision).
# Set to mmproj-F16.gguf and re-run scripts/download-model.sh to enable images.
MMPROJ_FILE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# llama-server tuning — sized for a 24 GB RTX 4090.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Context per slot. pi's own prompt is small — no MCP, no sub-agents, and with
# -nc no AGENTS.md discovery either — so nearly all of this is available to the
# session itself rather than being spent before the first message.
#
# 32768 -> 65536 (2026-08-22), on the back of CACHE_TYPE_K/V below. The old
# comment here said the cache "has to be f16/f16" because a quantized V cache
# takes prefill off the GPU. The measurement behind that was real; the
# diagnosis was one step short. It is not quantized V that breaks CUDA flash
# attention, it is a K type that differs from the V type — see CACHE_TYPE_K.
# With matched q8_0/q8_0 the KV cache halves and 64K fits with room to spare.
#
# The arithmetic, from the model card's own layout
# (16 x (3 x GatedDeltaNet -> FFN, 1 x GatedAttention -> FFN)) and the GGUF
# metadata (head_count_kv 4, key_length 256, value_length 256): only 16 of the
# 64 layers hold an attention KV cache at all — the other 48 are linear and
# keep a fixed-size recurrent state instead, which -ctk/-ctv do not touch.
# Counting the MTP block at blk.64 gives the 17 that llama.cpp#27109 reports
# for this architecture; 17 is used below so the estimate errs high.
#
#     f16/f16      17 layers x 4 heads x 256 x 2 (K+V) x 2 B  =  68 KiB/token
#     q8_0/q8_0    the same at ~1.0625 B/value                =  36 KiB/token
#
#     32K f16/f16  2.13 GiB      32K q8_0/q8_0  1.13 GiB
#     64K f16/f16  4.25 GiB      64K q8_0/q8_0  2.26 GiB
#     96K f16/f16  6.38 GiB      96K q8_0/q8_0  3.39 GiB
#
# So 64K at q8_0 costs ~130 MiB more than the 32K f16 window it replaces. The
# last measurement on this box was 21998 MiB of 24564 at 32K f16/f16 with the
# ngram-simple output buffers, i.e. ~2.5 GiB spare, so 64K is comfortably
# inside the card and 96K is plausible but unmeasured. Corroborated on the
# identical card and quant by a public 4090 run: 130K at q4_0 KV with MTP, and
# 170K at q8_0 KV without it — though see CACHE_TYPE_K on why the q4_0 half of
# that result is not available to us.
#
# RAISE THIS ONLY AFTER MEASURING. Compute buffers and the speculative output
# buffer (n_outputs_per_seq = 1 + ngram_simple.size_m = 49) also scale, and
# they are not in the table above. ./scripts/smoke-test.sh then ./scripts/bench.sh.
CTX_SIZE=98304
# Offload every layer. 999 = "all", clamped by llama.cpp to the real count.
NGL=999
# Flash attention: on | off | auto. Recent llama.cpp requires a VALUE here.
FLASH_ATTN=on

# KV cache element types. THESE TWO MUST MATCH. That is not a style rule, it is
# a hard gate in the CUDA backend of a stock llama.cpp build:
#
#     ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu, ggml_cuda_get_best_fattn_kernel():
#         #ifndef GGML_CUDA_FA_ALL_QUANTS
#             if (K->type != V->type) {
#                 return BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE;
#             }
#         #endif
#
# GGML_CUDA_FA_ALL_QUANTS is a compile-time option and the official
# ggml-org/llama.cpp CUDA images do not set it, so only the four matched pairs
# in the #else branch of ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_vec() are compiled:
# f16/f16, q4_0/q4_0, q8_0/q8_0, bf16/bf16 — each for head sizes 64, 128 and
# 256. This model's heads are 256 wide with key_length == value_length, which
# is exactly what the D=256 case requires, so q8_0/q8_0 is a supported kernel.
#
# This is what the earlier f16/q8_0 experiment actually hit. K f16 with V q8_0
# is a MISMATCHED pair, so it returned BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE, flash attention
# fell off the GPU, and prefill collapsed ~65x. The conclusion drawn at the
# time — "a quantized V cache takes prefill off the GPU" — reads the symptom
# correctly and the cause one step short: a matched quantized pair is fine.
# Turning flash attention off is not an escape either, because llama.cpp then
# refuses to start with "V cache quantization requires flash_attn".
#
# Confirmed upstream, not inferred from the source alone:
#
#   ggml-org/llama.cpp#20866 (CLOSED) "Asymmetric K/V cache quantization types
#       cannot be offloaded to GPU". A contributor's answer is explicit: the
#       mixed kernels exist but "aren't built by default. You need to rebuild
#       llama.cpp with -DGGML_CUDA_FA_ALL_QUANTS=ON". Another reporter measured
#       q8_0 K / q4_0 V at 96 tok/s prefill with the GPU at 0% and 180 MHz, and
#       2361 tok/s after rebuilding with the flag — a ~25x gap, the same shape
#       as the ~65x measured here. The thread's own summary: "users who don't
#       read build docs get silently terrible performance."
#
# q8_0 and NOT q4_0 or q4_1, and this one is a hard block rather than a
# preference:
#
#   ggml-org/llama.cpp#27109 (OPEN) "CUDA: 4-bit KV cache (q4_1/q4_0) collapses
#       prefill to ~34 t/s on qwen35 hybrid". Same architecture as this model —
#       the reporter describes it as "qwen35 hybrid ... 65 blocks, 17 attention
#       layers, GQA 4x256". Their measured table:
#
#           q8_0 / q8_0        991-1276 t/s prefill,  ~60 t/s generation
#           K=q4_1 / V=q8_0      34-106 t/s prefill,  ~60 t/s generation
#
#       and a 4-bit V alone reproduced it (195 t/s vs 1174 t/s). Generation is
#       untouched, so a decode-only benchmark will not catch this. The MMQ
#       shared-memory guard of #26141 is ruled out as the trigger; the bug is
#       open and unexplained. That makes the public 4090 recipes running
#       "-ctk q4_0 -ctv q4_0" for a 130K-250K window a prefill trap on this
#       architecture, however good their decode numbers look.
#
#       So: q4_0 is not "the cheaper option we declined for quality reasons",
#       it is known-broken here. q8_0/q8_0 is the only quantized pair this
#       model can currently use, and #27109's own numbers show it is the fast
#       path. Retest q4_0 only after #27109 closes.
#
# The hybrid layers are unaffected: llama_memory_hybrid takes type_k/type_v for
# the attention half and separate type_r/type_s for the recurrent half.
#
# VERIFY AFTER CHANGING: ./scripts/bench.sh must still report prefill in the
# thousands of tok/s. Three digits means flash attention went to the CPU again.
CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0

# --kv-tail-tokens — KEEP THE NEWEST N TOKENS OF THE KV CACHE EXACT while the
# body stays quantized. Empty = the flag is never passed, which is the only
# thing upstream llama.cpp would accept: THIS FLAG DOES NOT EXIST UPSTREAM. It
# is a beellama.cpp (Anbeeld) fork feature and is inert unless LLAMA_IMAGE above
# points at that fork. Setting it on an upstream image makes llama-server exit
# on an unrecognised argument, which presents as a container restarting with no
# model log at all.
#
# Why it is plumbed at all: the fork's own KLD ladder, measured on Qwen 3.6 27B
# — THIS attention layout — puts kvarn5/kvarn5 with a 1024 tail at a LOWER
# median KLD than the q8_0/q8_0 this stack runs today (0.000897 vs 0.000909) for
# 36.3% of the bf16 cache instead of 53.1%. Their q8_0 row reads 2176 MiB at
# 64K, which is byte-for-byte what vram_note measured off this engine at -lv 5,
# so their memory column is describing this cache and not a generic one.
#
# That arithmetic is what reopens ctx_128k_verdict, which is refused by 22 MiB.
# UNVERIFIED HERE: whether KVarN trips the llama.cpp#27109 prefill collapse on
# this hybrid. Their speed table is decode-only. Measure prefill before
# believing any of it.
KV_TAIL_TOKENS=
# Concurrent slots. 1 gives the single user the whole CTX_SIZE.
PARALLEL_SLOTS=1

# MTP speculative decoding. The GGUF carries a multi-token-prediction head that
# llama.cpp can use to draft tokens and verify them in one pass — decode speed
# only, output is identical. Set SPEC_TYPE empty to turn it off.
#
# On 3.6 this required a separate *-MTP-GGUF repo. On 3.8 it does not: the head
# ships in the mainline quants (see the MODEL_REPO note above). Check any
# third-party 3.8 GGUF before trusting it — abliteration and other transformers
# round trips drop blk.64 while config.json still advertises it, so a file can
# claim MTP and not have it. block_count 65 with blk.64.nextn.* present is the
# test; 64 blocks means no MTP and --spec-type draft-mtp will not help.
#
# n-max is the draft length: a public Qwen3.6 rig measured 2 as fastest, with 3
# no better and 4 collapsing back to non-MTP speed, and that is where this value
# came from. It is a 3.6 number and 3.8 is a different draft head — public 3.8
# reports on a 4090 run 4-5 with 47%/28% acceptance at the 4th/5th token, and
# the JonathanColetti card puts the llama.cpp default at 3. Re-measure with
# ./scripts/spec-sweep.sh before believing any of them, this one included.
# n-min: minimum draft tokens (0 = auto, lets engine fall back when acceptance
# is low). p-min: minimum per-token acceptance probability — higher values
# draft less aggressively.
#
# SWEEP p-min BEFORE n-max. Read at b10200 (common/speculative.cpp:1520-1670),
# the MTP draft loop tests the p-min gate BEFORE the n-max gate, so at
# p-min=0.75 the second token survives only when the head's top-1 probability
# clears 0.75 and n-max is frequently never reached. Raising n-max under a tight
# p-min therefore measures nothing, which is what the flat 2/3/4 results above
# actually are. 86.2% acceptance beside 39.6 tok/s is the signature: acceptance
# bought by refusing to draft, not earned by drafting well. spec-sweep.sh
# reports draft/cycle, which tells the two apart.
#
# MEASURED on this box, 2026-08-16, draft-mtp only (decode tok/s, draft/cycle):
#
#   config              novel text        repetitive text
#   n2 p0.75 (this)     55.1   1.30       74.7   1.95
#   n2 p0.50            59.5   1.70       73.4   1.99
#   n2 p0.40            63.6   1.84       85.1   2.00
#   n4 p0.40            67.9   3.48      118.1   3.98
#
# draft/cycle sits hard against the n-max ceiling in every row, so n-max is
# still binding at 4 and 6/8 is untested. The best measured MTP-only config is
# n-max 4 / p-min 0.40: +23% on novel text, +39% on repetitive.
#
# ADOPTED 2026-08-17: the values below are the measured optimum of the full 2x6
# grid, not the inherited 3.6 defaults. Against the old draft-mtp / n2 / p0.75
# they are 1.23x on novel text and 2.20x on repetitive. Speculative decoding is
# output-identical by construction — every draft is verified against the target
# model — so this buys decode speed and changes nothing the model says.
# To revert: draft-mtp / 2 / 0.75, and re-read the tables above first.
#
# SPEC_TYPE is a comma-separated LIST (common/arg.cpp:4048), not one value, and
# the engine hard-codes the priority (common/speculative.cpp:2404-2437): every
# n-gram drafter runs ahead of every draft-model one, first-non-empty wins. So
#     SPEC_TYPE=ngram-simple,draft-mtp
# tries a free lookup over the live context first (no llama_decode at all, up to
# size_m=48 tokens per draft, defaults in common/common.h:358) and falls back to
# MTP only on a miss.
#
# IT IS ONLY SAFE AT n-max 4. Measured here 2026-08-16/17, both workloads, full
# 2x6 grid, decode tok/s:
#
#   config                              novel   repetitive
#   draft-mtp            n2 p0.75        55.1        74.7   <- current
#   draft-mtp            n4 p0.40        67.9       118.1
#   ngram-simple+mtp     n2 p0.75        41.1       152.5   <- 25% LOSS on novel
#   ngram-simple+mtp     n4 p0.40        67.8       164.4   <- no loss, +39% rep
#
# At n-max 2 adding ngram-simple costs 25% on novel text. The priority cascade
# is why: ngram-simple runs ahead of draft-mtp and arbitration is first-non-
# empty, so any span the n-gram table produces PREEMPTS the MTP draft, including
# a bad guess (draft/cycle 1.30 -> 1.45 while acceptance falls 89.8% -> 76.3% —
# it fires and misses). At n-max 2 the MTP draft it displaces is short and cheap,
# so a bad guess is pure loss.
#
# At n-max 4 that reverses: the draft budget is wide enough that the spans which
# land pay for the ones that do not (draft/cycle 3.48 -> 3.79, acceptance 54.5%
# -> 51.8%), and novel-text decode is 67.8 vs 67.9 — identical within noise.
#
# So `ngram-simple,draft-mtp` at n-max 4 / p-min 0.40 is a STRICT improvement
# over the current values on both workloads: 1.23x novel, 2.20x repetitive.
# Enabling ngram-simple WITHOUT also raising n-max is the trap.
#
# Untested lever: --spec-ngram-simple-min-hits defaults to 1 (common/common.h
# :361) — a span is drafted after being seen ONCE. 2-3 may add headroom. It is
# plumbed through docker-compose.yml as SPEC_NGRAM_MIN_HITS below, default empty.
#
# Re-measure with BOTH workloads before changing this line:
#   ./scripts/spec-sweep.sh                    # novel  — the pessimistic case
#   ./scripts/spec-sweep.sh --workload repeat  # repetitive — the optimistic one
# See context/design/decisions.md, 2026-08-16.
SPEC_TYPE=ngram-simple,draft-mtp
SPEC_DRAFT_N_MAX=4
SPEC_DRAFT_N_MIN=0
SPEC_DRAFT_P_MIN=0.40

# ngram-simple tuning. Empty = llama.cpp's own defaults (size-n 12, size-m 48,
# min-hits 1 — common/common.h:358-362); each flag is only passed when set, so
# an empty value here is not the same as writing the default in.
#
# min-hits is the one that matters: at 1, a span is drafted after being seen
# ONCE, which is what makes ngram-simple lose 25% on novel text (it preempts the
# MTP draft with a guess it has no evidence for). 2-3 is the untested fix.
# Inert unless SPEC_TYPE contains ngram-simple.
SPEC_NGRAM_MIN_HITS=
SPEC_NGRAM_SIZE_N=
SPEC_NGRAM_SIZE_M=

# --- speculative DRAFT MODEL (draft-dflash / draft-dspark / draft-eagle3) -----
#
# PLUMBING ONLY. THE VERDICT ON DFLASH2 IS STILL NO, and these keys stay empty.
# Added 2026-08-30 so that the day the blocker below clears, measuring it is an
# .env edit and a sweep arm rather than a compose change. Production stays
# ngram-simple,draft-mtp at n-max 4 / p-min 0.40, which IS measured here.
#
# draft-mtp needs no file here: its head ships inside the main GGUF. These keys
# exist for the spec types that need a SECOND model on disk.
#
# WHY DFLASH2 IS TEMPTING. Block-diffusion draft: a whole block of draft tokens
# per forward pass, target hidden states injected into the draft's attention.
# Merged as b10f9ca5 (#27342 / #27816, 2026-08-27), so it needs b10689+. The
# author's eval on Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M, block 8, reports 1.81x decode at
# acceptance 5.03 with a Q4_K_M drafter (10.42 -> 18.89 tok/s). That is the
# NOVEL-TEXT case, which is exactly the leg where this stack falls back to
# draft-mtp (spec_verdict: ngram-simple carries repeat at 191 tok/s, mtp-n4 is
# the 120.2 fallback). So the shape of the win would be real.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL NO, VERIFIED AT THE SOURCE AT b10689 AND NOT INFERRED FROM
# THE SYMPTOM. The DFlash2 drafter keeps its OWN KV cache, filled incrementally
# as each prefill ubatch passes through process(). When the server reuses a
# cached prompt prefix it SKIPS prefill for those tokens, so process() never
# runs for them and the draft cache has a hole at those positions. The merged
# code detects this and only warns — common/speculative.cpp @ b10689,
# common_speculative_impl_draft_dflash::begin():
#
#     const llama_pos pos_max = llama_memory_seq_pos_max(...);
#     if (pos_max < N - 1) {
#         LOG_WRN("%s: ctx_dft pos_max=%d < N-1=%d - process() did not run on "
#                 "every prefill ubatch. Drafts may degrade.\n", ...);
#     }
#
# There is no backfill, no replay of prompt[pos_max+1 .. N-1], and `n_swa` /
# `is_masked_swa` appear NOWHERE in that file — so the bounded ~2048-row fix
# proposed on the PR (#27342, laidick 2026-08-22) was not taken. process() also
# still carries `TODO: revisit after #24669 is merged` for the embedding-batch
# form of the same hole, and #24669 (llama_batch_ext) is OPEN.
#
# THIS STACK IS THE MAXIMALLY EXPOSED CASE, not a bystander: CACHE_PROMPT=1,
# CACHE_RAM=2048, CACHE_REUSE=64, SLOT_PROMPT_SIMILARITY=0.20, and an agent
# workload whose every turn re-sends a long shared prefix of tool-result
# history. That is the precise trigger. The 4090 report in the LLAMA_TAG note
# above — 60-105 -> 19-20 tok/s by ~28k output on 100-message tool histories,
# 12-14 tok/s from ~200 output tokens on a real ~200-message session, draft-mtp
# and no-spec immune on the identical request — is this mechanism presenting.
# A claim on the PR thread that this was "fixed as of f7aadef" does not survive
# reading the merged file: the warn-only branch above is what actually shipped.
#
# RETRACTED, so do not carry it forward as a reason: the ~13% MTP decode
# regression reported in this PR's window (cobra91, RTX 5080, NVFP4) was
# withdrawn by its own reporter on 2026-08-27 after a greedy warmup-discarded
# re-run (188 pre-window vs 187 at head). It was a cold-start artefact — first
# generation after load runs ~10% slow. It is NOT an argument against b10689.
#
# REVISIT WHEN, concretely — one of:
#   * begin() gains a real backfill (replay the missing range through
#     process(), ideally bounded to the draft's sliding window), or
#   * the false-return path degrades to unspeculated generation instead of
#     failing the request, or
#   * #24669 lands and the embedding-hole TODO is closed.
# Then: download the drafter, add the sweep arms, and measure on BOTH workloads
# before believing any of the numbers above.
#
# WHEN THAT DAY COMES, the settings to start from:
#   DRAFT_MODEL_REPO=incoai/Qwen3.8-27B-DFlash2-GGUF
#   DRAFT_GGUF_FILE=Qwen3.8-27B-DFlash2-Q4_K_M.gguf
#   SPEC_TYPE=ngram-simple,draft-dflash
#   SPEC_DRAFT_N_MAX=8
# incoai, not z-lab: #27342 warns that GGUFs generated before 2026-08-27 need
# reconversion; z-lab last moved 08-24, incoai 08-29. The caveat is vision-only
# and MMPROJ_FILE is empty here, so it cannot bite — there is just no reason to
# take the older bytes. n-max is CLAMPED to the trained block size, 8 here, so
# the current 4 would leave half the block unused.
#
# VRAM, for when it is measured. docs/quants.md budgets ~22.4 GiB usable.
# UD-Q4_K_XL 16.4 + q8_0 KV at 96K ~3.19 + CUDA context ~1.3 = ~20.9 GiB, so
# ~1.5 GiB spare against a 1.14 GB (1.06 GiB) Q4_K_M drafter plus its own KV.
# FITS ON PAPER AND ONLY ON PAPER — ./scripts/capacity-probe.sh decides. The
# Q8_0 drafter (2.06 GB) does not fit at this context.
#
# OTHER ARMS, same blocker applies (they share begin()/process()):
# RadixArk/Qwen3.8-27B-DSpark (GGUF via magnitudedev, erlidev) — llama.cpp's own
# loader says "dspark outranks dflash, its sidecar carries the extra Markov
# head", and it adds --spec-draft-conf-min. No EAGLE3 checkpoint exists for
# Qwen3.8-27B (searched the Hub 2026-08-30: zero hits).
#
# Both keys must be set or both empty — the downloader refuses a half-set pair,
# because a filename with no repo would be looked for in the TARGET's repo where
# a near-namesake may well exist and load.
DRAFT_MODEL_REPO=
DRAFT_GGUF_FILE=

# The draft's OWN KV cache type. Empty = llama.cpp's default; these do NOT
# inherit CACHE_TYPE_K/V. The matched-pair rule applies here too (a mismatched
# pair returns BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE — see CACHE_TYPE_K above), so set both or
# neither. SPEC_DRAFT_NGL empty = auto, which offloads the whole draft.
SPEC_DRAFT_CACHE_TYPE_K=
SPEC_DRAFT_CACHE_TYPE_V=
SPEC_DRAFT_NGL=

# Thinking budget in tokens: -1 unrestricted, 0 disables thinking entirely,
# N>0 caps it. Unrestricted thinking is what hangs runs / fills the KV cache on
# reasoning-tagged models, so this is capped by default.
REASONING_BUDGET=4096

# How hard 3.8 thinks. NEW IN 3.8 — 3.6 had no such control. Passed to the chat
# template via --chat-template-kwargs in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Accepted values, read out of the template embedded in the GGUF rather than
# from anyone's docs: xhigh | high | medium | low. `high` is silently rewritten
# to `xhigh`. `medium` is the only level that injects NOTHING into the system
# prompt; xhigh and low each prepend a steering sentence. Anything else — and
# that includes the `none` some write-ups list — hits raise_exception() and the
# request fails, so do not put an empty value HERE.
#
# CORRECTION 2026-08-17: that last sentence is true for THIS setting (which goes
# to the template via --chat-template-kwargs) and false for the API. llama.cpp
# intercepts the OpenAI-style top-level field before the template ever sees it
# (tools/server/server-common.cpp:1089-1094):
#
#     if (reasoning_effort == "none") { inputs.enable_thinking = false; }
#     // other reasoning_effort values are model-specific and not yet handled
#
# so a request body carrying {"reasoning_effort": "none"} disables thinking
# cleanly instead of raising. Measured through this stack: 0 reasoning chars vs
# 97 on the same prompt. Only "none" is special-cased — low/medium/xhigh must go
# via chat_template_kwargs.
#
# PER-REQUEST OVERRIDE WORKS, AND FORGE FORWARDS IT (measured 2026-08-17).
# server-common.cpp:1073-1077 merges a request's chat_template_kwargs OVER the
# --chat-template-kwargs set here, so a caller can pick its own effort per turn:
#
#     {"chat_template_kwargs": {"reasoning_effort": "low"}}
#     {"reasoning_effort": "none"}          # thinking off entirely
#
# Verified end to end on the same prompt (content length through forge tracked
# llama within 1%), so the "drop to low for tool-loop turns" note below is a
# per-request client change, NOT a server restart.
#
# medium, not upstream's xhigh default. xhigh prepends "Reasoning effort is set
# to xhigh. Please think carefully through the task, validate key assumptions,
# consider plausible alternatives..." and the model obliges: the public reports
# on release day are 20-90 minute single answers and 22-36k thinking tokens for
# one SVG. Against REASONING_BUDGET=4096 that is not "slower", it is a budget
# cut-off on every turn — the reply arrives mid-thought with the budget message
# stapled on. xhigh needs REASONING_BUDGET raised into the tens of thousands and
# a context window to hold it, which a 32K window on one 4090 does not have.
#
# Raise it to xhigh for a hard one-shot problem, with REASONING_BUDGET raised to
# match. For tool-loop turns, send {"reasoning_effort":"none"} per request (see
# the correction above) rather than changing this.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-17 with ./scripts/bench_quality.py — 5 coding tasks, 5 hidden
# edge-case assertions each, model code EXECUTED rather than eyeballed:
#
#   effort   pass%    LOC   reason_chars     wall
#   none      84.0    164              0    23.3s
#   low       96.0     63          9,007    48.3s
#   medium   100.0     71         13,876    59.9s
#   xhigh    100.0     99         41,489   244.4s
#
# Read LOC as OVER-ENGINEERING, which is what the xhigh complaint is actually
# about — not slowness. xhigh ties medium on correctness while writing 40% more
# code (39 lines for roman_to_int where low used 13) and burning 4x the wall
# clock: it buys nothing here. `none` is worst on both axes at once — fewest
# passes AND the most sprawling code, because with no planning phase it rambles.
#
# medium stays the default: it is the only level at 100%, and a coding agent's
# failure mode is a wrong function, not a verbose one. `low` is defensible if
# you weigh terseness above the last 4 points — it writes the leanest correct
# code of any level.
#
# Also measured, and it does NOT generalise: on one HTML/UI prompt `low` reasoned
# MORE than `medium` (6,409 vs 3,404 chars) while across these five coding tasks
# it reasons LESS (9,007 vs 13,876). Effort level is not a monotonic dial on
# thinking volume — it depends on the prompt. Do not extrapolate either way from
# a single prompt, which an earlier version of this comment did.
#
# Separately: at a token cap, xhigh can spend the ENTIRE budget thinking and
# return an empty content field — a failed turn, not a slow one. Measured at
# 12,582 reasoning chars with 0 content. That is what REASONING_BUDGET guards.
#
# TWO PUBLIC CLAIMS ABOUT THIS MODEL, 2026-08-24 HN thread on the XDA
# reverse-engineering piece, checked against the measurements above rather than
# adopted or dismissed:
#
#   "reasoning effort set to low doesn't save you tokens: low is pretty
#    uncertain about things so it ends up thinking more"        (Refefer)
# CORROBORATED, and this file already said it more precisely. Across the five
# coding tasks low reasons LESS (9,007 vs 13,876 chars); on one HTML/UI prompt
# it reasoned MORE (6,409 vs 3,404). The claim is true on some prompts and false
# on others, which is exactly the non-monotonicity recorded above. Nothing to
# change; do not let the HN phrasing flatten it back into a rule.
#
#   "adding reasoning budget really hurts the model. you need to let it spin
#    for as many thinking tokens as it wants"                   (Refefer)
# DOES NOT BIND HERE, measured. At medium, three prompts produced 77 / 900 / 636
# chars of reasoning against a 4096-TOKEN budget — the budget is never reached,
# so it cannot be shortening anything. It binds at xhigh, which is precisely
# what the paragraphs above already say and why medium is the default. The
# opposite tip in the same thread (jnwatson: --reasoning-budget 8000 +
# --reasoning-budget-message + a low effort level "produces pretty good output")
# is this stack's existing configuration, arrived at independently.
REASONING_EFFORT=medium

# Language the model reasons in. off | zh
# Set by scripts/mode.sh: `coding` uses zh, `prose` uses off (the fragment is a
# page about verbatim tool arguments and code identifiers, which buys fiction
# nothing). Enabled for coding on 2026-08-11 by operator decision, ahead of
# measurement, and still unmeasured. From the HN
# thread of the same date (dannyw): Qwen's thinking is claimed to be strongest
# and most token-efficient in Mandarin, because that is what it was most
# natively trained on. The follow-up in that thread — "better thinking, or just
# shorter?" (kadoban) — was never answered and nobody posted a number, so this
# is running on a community claim rather than a local result.
#
# Nothing enforces it server-side: neither llama-server nor forge can inject a
# system prompt (see prompts/README.md), so the launchers append
# prompts/think-<lang>.md client-side.
#
# Confirm it on this hardware, and record the numbers:
#   ./scripts/ab-think-lang.sh --repeat 3 --save
# Back it out with one edit — THINK_LANG=off — if tool arguments come back with
# Chinese in them, which is the failure that breaks edits and file paths.
THINK_LANG=zh

# Sampling. Qwen3.8 publishes ONE thinking-mode preset — temperature 1.0 — where
# 3.6 published two (1.0 general, 0.6 "precise coding"). The 0.6 this stack ran
# for coding has no successor in the 3.8 card, and the GGUF itself carries
# general.sampling.temp=1.0 / top_k=20 / top_p=0.95 as its stated defaults, so
# both modes now sit at 1.0. Client requests override either.
#
# This block is rewritten by scripts/mode.sh. Do not describe the current regime
# in a comment here — the values move and the prose does not, which is exactly
# how this file ended up claiming to be "SET FOR PROSE" while holding the coding
# temperature. `./scripts/mode.sh` reports the live answer; modes/*.env holds the
# per-regime values and the reasoning for each.
TEMPERATURE=1.0
TOP_P=0.95
TOP_K=20
MIN_P=0.0

# --- creative samplers -------------------------------------------------------
# At llama.cpp's own "disabled" values, which is what `coding` mode sets — so
# `coding` is Qwen's published preset and nothing else.
# `prose` turns DRY on. Also rewritten by scripts/mode.sh — see the note above
# about not asserting the current regime in this file.
#
# The sampler chain this build runs, in order:
#   penalties -> dry -> top_n_sigma -> top_k -> typ_p -> top_p -> min_p -> xtc -> temperature
#
# XTC ("exclude top choices") removes the most-probable tokens when several are
# plausible, which is the one sampler here aimed squarely at stopping prose from
# collapsing into the same phrasing. Start at probability 0.5 / threshold 0.1.
XTC_PROBABILITY=0.0
XTC_THRESHOLD=0.1

# DRY penalises repeated *sequences* rather than repeated tokens. Prefer it to
# REPEAT_PENALTY for prose: a flat repeat penalty also punishes ordinary
# function words and flattens style, which is the problem KoboldCpp's
# "smoothing_factor" is usually reached for. llama.cpp has no quadratic/
# smoothing sampler, so DRY is the equivalent lever here. 0.8 is a normal start.
#
# `prose` sets 0.8 as a deliberate SUBSTITUTION, not a card value. The card wants
# smoothing_factor 1.5 (KoboldCpp-only, absent from llama.cpp) or repeat pen
# 1.1-1.15 (which the same card forbids on MTP quants). DRY is the only
# repetition control here that satisfies both. Zero it to run pure card values
# and judge the difference. Costs nothing measurable: 42.6 vs 44.6 tok/s decode
# over three runs each, which is noise.
DRY_MULTIPLIER=0.0
DRY_BASE=1.75
DRY_ALLOWED_LENGTH=2
# 2026-08-22: was -1. llama.cpp removed the "-1 = context size" sentinel
# between b10200 and b10573 — b10200 declared
# `int32_t dry_penalty_last_n = -1; // 0 = disable penalty, -1 = context size`
# and rejected only value < -1; b10573 declares the default as 64 and rejects
# ANY negative. With -1 still set, llama-server exits during argument parsing
# with `error: invalid dry-penalty-last-n = -1` and the container crash-loops
# before it ever reaches the model, which is how this was found.
#
# 65536 is the faithful port of what -1 meant: scan the whole context. It must
# track CTX_SIZE — if CTX_SIZE changes and this does not, DRY silently stops
# scanning the tail of the window. Only `prose` is affected in practice, since
# `coding` runs DRY_MULTIPLIER=0.0 and the scan length is then unused.
DRY_PENALTY_LAST_N=98304

# Flat penalties. The model card asks for repeat pen 1.1-1.15 for chat/roleplay
# *but* also says keep it at 1.0 on MTP quants or performance suffers — the two
# pieces of advice conflict, and that conflict is itself a reason to run the
# regular (non-MTP) GGUF for prose. Left at 1.0 (disabled).
REPEAT_PENALTY=1.0
REPEAT_LAST_N=64
PRESENCE_PENALTY=0.0
FREQUENCY_PENALTY=0.0

# Dynamic temperature and top-n-sigma. Both disabled; -1.0 disables sigma.
DYNATEMP_RANGE=0.0
DYNATEMP_EXP=1.0
TOP_N_SIGMA=-1.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prompt cache & KV checkpointing — from pierotofy/LocalCodingLLM (27★),
# sasasin/dotfiles, rcarmo/llama-cpp, and loops-and-spells/pi-setup.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Persist the KV cache to disk so warm restarts skip re-prefill. Set to 0 to
# disable (saves the RAM budget below but loses fast-restart).
CACHE_PROMPT=1
# RAM budget for the prompt cache in MiB. Upstream default: 8192.
#
# 2048, not 8192: this is HOST RAM, and 8192 was the single largest avoidable
# consumer on this box. Measured 2026-08-13 — the llama container sat at
# 9.02 GiB RSS (8 GiB cache + ~1 GiB overhead) on a 22 GiB VM shared with the
# other containers and a dozen agent sessions, which pushed the host to 394 MiB
# free with 5.5 GiB in swap. Prompt processing collapsed from ~2000 tok/s to
# **2.83 tok/s** under that pressure — the same symptom as the KV-quant bug,
# from a completely different cause, which is why the number is recorded here.
# 0 disables the cache entirely (losing warm restarts), -1 removes the limit.
CACHE_RAM=2048
# Reuse previous prompt cache entries. Higher = more cache hits but more CPU
# work per comparison. Engine default: 0 (disabled). sasasin config: 128.
#
# NOTE: this does nothing on this model. llama-server logs
#   "cache_reuse is not supported by this context, it will be disabled"
# at startup — the hybrid Gated DeltaNet layers carry recurrent state that
# cannot be partially reused. Left here because it is harmless and because the
# next model may support it; do not count it as an optimisation that is running.
CACHE_REUSE=64
# Prompt similarity threshold for cache reuse (0.0–1.0). Lower = more cache
# hits. sasasin config: 0.20. Only effective with CACHE_REUSE > 0.
SLOT_PROMPT_SIMILARITY=0.20
# Number of KV cache checkpoints per slot. Higher = more rewind points for
# agentic loops at the cost of VRAM. 0 = disable. Upstream default: 32.
# sasasin config: 24 with checkpoint-min-step 256.
CTX_CHECKPOINTS=16
# Minimum tokens between checkpoints. Smaller = more granular rewind.
# Upstream default: 8192. The sasasin config uses 256 for agentic workloads.
CHECKPOINT_MIN_STEP=256
# Custom message the engine emits when the reasoning budget is exhausted.
# Cleaner than the default — tells the model to stop and deliver.
REASONING_BUDGET_MESSAGE=Reasoning budget reached. Stop thinking and provide the final answer now.

# Appended verbatim to the llama-server command line. Whitespace-separated.
#
# NO KV QUANTIZATION. This was `-ctk f16 -ctv q8_0` and it was costing ~65x on
# every prompt, measured on this hardware 2026-08-12:
#
#   -ctk f16 -ctv q8_0   prompt processing 26-140 tok/s and FALLING with prompt
#                        length (16s, 43s, 86s, 172s per 2048-token chunk —
#                        doubling), GPU utilisation 0%, an 8K-token request
#                        never finishing inside forge's 600s backend timeout
#   f16 / f16 (this)     prompt processing 1806 tok/s, the same 12K-token
#                        request answered correctly in 6.9s
#
# The old comment here claimed this model's 256-wide heads are in the CUDA
# flash-attention head-size table "regardless of cache quant". That is false on
# b10200: with a quantized V cache, prefill leaves the GPU. And the combination
# cannot be avoided by turning flash attention off either — llama.cpp refuses to
# start: "V cache quantization requires flash_attn".
#
# The cost is context. f16/f16 KV is ~132 KiB/token against ~98 KiB/token for
# f16/q8_0, which is why CTX_SIZE came down from 65536 to 32768 to fit the card.
# A 32K window that answers in seconds beats a 64K window that times out.
#
# -b/-ub are NOT set. They were `-b 4096 -ub 2048`, which cost ~850 MiB of VRAM
# for a prompt-processing win that does not exist once prefill is on the GPU.
# --load-mode none disables mmap, and on this machine it is the difference
# between loading and not loading. MODELS_DIR is a Docker Desktop bind mount,
# which is 9p: demand-paging the GGUF through it was measured at ~0.05 MB/s
# (the process sits in D state, wchan p9_client_rpc, and never finishes),
# against 12-38 MB/s for plain sequential reads of the same file. --no-mmap is
# the older spelling of this and llama.cpp b10200 warns that it is deprecated.
# Drop this only if MODELS_DIR ever becomes a real Linux filesystem.
#
# -n is a server-side generation cap and a real backstop, not decoration: a
# request that arrives without n_predict falls back to this global value
# (tools/server/server-context.cpp — global_params.n_predict is consulted when
# the task did not set one). Any client or proxy that sends the newer
# `max_completion_tokens` instead of `max_tokens` gets no cap at all, because
# llama.cpp reads only the latter and copies unknown keys through unused — so
# this is the backstop that makes the limit independent of what the client
# sends. Keep it equal to PI_MAX_TOKENS.
LLAMA_EXTRA_FLAGS=-n 8192 --load-mode none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# forge proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# FORGE 0.9.0 (2026-08-15, up from 0.8.2). Three contract changes matter here,
# all verified against a running 0.9.0 rather than read off the migration doc:
#
#   1. --budget-mode is GONE from the compose command. An externally managed
#      backend (--backend-url) is "unmanaged", and 0.9 rejects budget_mode for
#      unmanaged backends outright: "error: unmanaged backends do not accept
#      budget_mode", exit 2, the proxy never starts. --budget-tokens stays and
#      is now a reporting denominator only.
#   2. The proxy is unconditionally no-compaction. It never trims or drops
#      caller history any more. That is not a loss here: pi does its own
#      context management, PI_MAX_TOKENS sits well under CTX_SIZE, and
#      --no-context-shift means an overflow fails loudly instead of silently
#      sliding the window.
#   3. /health now forwards the BACKEND's readiness; forge's own liveness moved
#      to /forge/health. The compose healthcheck and smoke_test.py both follow
#      it. Probing /health instead would report forge as unhealthy for the whole
#      ~24 minute cold load of a model that is loading normally.
#
# The build-time patch in patches/forge_merge_consecutive.py still applies to
# 0.9.0 — the structured-content merge bug is unfixed upstream, and the patch
# fails the image build loudly if the source line ever moves.

# native = forward the client's tools to llama.cpp's --jinja function calling.
# prompt = prompt-injection fallback (only for backends without native FC).
FORGE_CAPABILITY=native
FORGE_MAX_RETRIES=0
FORGE_MAX_TOOL_ERRORS=2
# THESE TWO ARE ONE SETTING, and the pair used to have a silent dead end in it.
# FORGE_MAX_RETRIES bounds forge's ATTEMPT loop (attempt_limit = retries + 1);
# FORGE_MAX_TOOL_ERRORS bounds one nudge kind, `tool_arg_validation` — a tool
# call whose arguments do not decode to a dict, which is what a call cut off at
# the token cap always looks like. With 0 and 2, the second outlived the first:
# the first malformed call was "not exhausted", so forge appended a correction
# for an attempt it had no budget to make, fell out of its own loop and returned
# None — which the proxy handed to the client as an empty 200 with no content, no
# usage, no finish_reason and NO LOG LINE. Measured 2026-08-27 on a live /loop
# run: 20+ consecutive turns of exactly 8,192 generated tokens (~85 s of GPU
# each) delivered to pi as `content: []` reported as a natural "stop", 45 minutes
# of it, zero files changed. patches/forge_empty_turn.py closes the dead end —
# exhaustion is now whichever budget runs out first — so the pair is safe to
# leave as it is. Raising FORGE_MAX_RETRIES re-arms the nudge loop the comment
# above rejects; lowering FORGE_MAX_TOOL_ERRORS changes nothing while retries
# are 0.
# 0 retries, deliberately. forge treats a bare text reply as a validation
# failure whenever the request carries tools, and each retry appends a nudge
# ("Your previous response was not a valid tool call") and re-runs inference.
# In an agent loop that fires on the majority of turns — after a tool result
# comes back, answering in text IS correct — so every retry is pure waste on a
# response that was already right.
#
# With 0, forge makes one attempt, raises ToolCallError, and passes the text
# through unchanged. Rescue parsing still runs, so a malformed tool call is
# still recovered; only the nudge-and-retry loop is gone, and pi has its own
# agentic loop for anything that genuinely needs another turn.
#
# Measured 2026-08-13, same two request shapes through three configs:
#   max-retries 3, no respond tool  tool turn 3.6s | answer turn 6.7s WRONG
#                                   (returned another tool call, not the answer)
#   max-retries 3, respond tool on  tool turn 1.3s | answer turn 5.1s correct
#   max-retries 0 (this)            tool turn 1.7s | answer turn 0.8s correct
# none | keep-last | full — how much captured reasoning is replayed backend-side.
# Set to full (was none) per HN thread finding: preserve_thinking on the engine
# side avoids a full KV re-prefill every agentic turn. Paired with
# --chat-template-kwargs '{"preserve_thinking": true}' in docker-compose.yml.
FORGE_REASONING_REPLAY=full
# Generous: a 27B on one 4090 with a 4k thinking budget can take a while.
FORGE_BACKEND_TIMEOUT=600
# Appended verbatim to the forge command line. Useful additions:
#   --inject-respond-tool   makes the model answer via respond(message="..."),
#                           which also fixes the nudge loop FORGE_MAX_RETRIES=0
#                           addresses — but measured slower (5.1s vs 0.8s on an
#                           answer turn), because every answer is then wrapped
#                           in a JSON tool call the model has to generate.
#   -v                      debug logging
# Merge two same-role messages that are separated by a tool-call/tool-result
# pair? Upstream forge always does, as a workaround for llama-server's MISTRAL
# Jinja template, whose parity checker counts only plain user/assistant messages.
# Qwen3.8's template has no such constraint — checked against the live server's
# /apply-template — and on an agent loop that merge folds every new user turn
# into the FIRST user message, rewriting the prompt PREFIX every turn.
#
# Measured, cache_n straight from llama, three turns, one nonce per arm:
#   no forge at all      0/395   391/517   513/702    reuse GROWS with the turn
#   forge, merge ON      0/396   349/582   349/754    pinned where the rewrite starts
#   forge, merge OFF     0/396   392/518   514/703    matches the no-forge baseline
#
# 0 is therefore the right default HERE and the patch that makes it possible is
# patches/forge_merge_across_tools.py. Set to 1 if you ever point this stack at a
# backend whose template does count visible alternation — the symptom there is a
# 500 on the first user turn after a tool result.
# Adjacent same-role merges (the retry nudge) happen either way.
FORGE_MERGE_ACROSS_TOOLS=0
FORGE_EXTRA_FLAGS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measurement — what is left after the eval harness was removed (2026-08-15).
#
# The scored eval suite, its committed scorecard, its badges and the Harbor
# adapter are gone from this repo. What verifies the stack now:
#   ./scripts/smoke-test.sh       does it work at all, end to end
#   ./scripts/bench.sh            prefill / decode throughput
#   ./scripts/ab-think-lang.sh    THINK_LANG fragment, with thinking forced on
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-request timeouts, in seconds.
AB_TIMEOUT=900
BENCH_TIMEOUT=900

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Networking
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 127.0.0.1 keeps both services host-only. Use 0.0.0.0 to expose on the LAN.
BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
LLAMA_PORT=8080
FORGE_PORT=8081

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workstream capture (scripts/capture.sh, scripts/capture_proxy.py)
#
# OFF by default. Nothing below changes anything until FORGE_BACKEND_URL points
# at the capture container, which `./scripts/capture.sh on` does for you.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. `context/design/inference-divergence-and-this-stack.md` §7
# names "start keeping real workstream captures" as the item every remaining
# fidelity experiment is gated on — the q8_0-vs-f16 KLD run in particular, whose
# answer is worthless on synthetic filler because the article's own strongest
# secondary finding is that top-1 disagreement is PROMPT-DEPENDENT.
#
# WHERE THE TAPE IS TAKEN, and why the default is where it is. With the capture
# container between forge and llama, the tape is what the MODEL SAW: pi's system
# prompt, the tool schemas, forge's reasoning replay and its _merge_consecutive
# rewrite, all already applied. Point CAPTURE_UPSTREAM at forge instead and it
# records what the AGENT ASKED FOR, which is a different and much less useful
# thing for a fidelity measurement — the two are labelled in every line so a
# tape can never be read as the wrong one.
#
# COST. One extra hop inside the compose network, and the tape's own bytes.
# Requests carry the whole conversation, so a session's tape grows quadratically
# in its turns: a 200-turn session at 90k tokens is roughly 80 MB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where forge sends requests. Normally llama directly; capture.sh flips it to
# the capture container and back. A forge restart is seconds and costs nothing —
# the KV cache lives in llama, which does not move.
FORGE_BACKEND_URL=http://llama:8080
# Host directory for the tape. A Docker Desktop bind mount, so it must be the
# WINDOWS-side path — same rule as MODELS_DIR, and for the same reason: a
# WSL-style source silently mounts an EMPTY directory here rather than failing.
CAPTURES_DIR=//d/llm-captures
# Where the recorder sends what it records, i.e. WHICH SIDE it sits on.
#   http://llama:8080   forge -> capture -> llama   the model-facing tape
#   http://forge:8081   pi    -> capture -> forge   the client-facing tape
# `capture.sh on --position client-forge` sets both this and CAPTURE_POSITION
# for you, and does NOT repoint forge — in that position the CLIENT moves, not
# the backend.
CAPTURE_UPSTREAM=http://llama:8080
# What the tape IS. Recorded in every line; the reader refuses to guess.
CAPTURE_POSITION=forge-llama
# pi's own session transcripts, mounted READ-ONLY into the `sessions` service so
# `capture.sh import-pi` can read them. It has to be the WINDOWS-side path, same
# rule as MODELS_DIR and CAPTURES_DIR: a WSL-style source silently mounts an
# EMPTY directory on Docker Desktop rather than failing. Here ~/ is a 9p mount of
# C:\users\user\downloads\as\data, so ~/.pi/agent/sessions is the path below.
# capture.sh rewrites a host path under it to the container's /pi-sessions, so
# the command you type is the one in the README.
# Change it if PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR moves pi's agent directory.
PI_SESSIONS_DIR=//c/users/user/downloads/as/data/.pi/agent/sessions
# Published for the client-facing position only, so pi has somewhere to point.
# Bound to BIND_ADDR like everything else: a tape recorder reachable from the
# LAN is a transcript leak.
CAPTURE_PORT=8082
# Recording stops (proxying does not) past this many bytes, counting what is
# already in CAPTURES_DIR. 8 GiB is roughly a hundred deep sessions.
CAPTURE_MAX_BYTES=8589934592

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pi (scripts/pi-local.sh) — the only client this stack targets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generation cap advertised to pi as maxTokens, and the value LLAMA_EXTRA_FLAGS
# mirrors in -n. Deliberately well under CTX_SIZE: with --no-context-shift a
# request that would overflow fails loudly, and an agent loop's prompt grows
# every turn, so the reply has to leave room for the turn after it.
PI_MAX_TOKENS=8192
# 1 loads the project's AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md; 0 passes -nc, which skips that
# discovery entirely.
#
# ON by default. It was off to save window, and that was the wrong trade: a
# coding agent that ignores the conventions file sitting in the repo it is
# editing produces work you then have to correct by hand, which costs far more
# than the tokens. pi walks parent directories to find them, so the cost scales
# with how much you have written down, not with the size of the repo.
#
# Set to 0 for a session where the window matters more than the conventions —
# a long single-file refactor, or a project whose AGENTS.md is enormous.
PI_CONTEXT_FILES=1
# Shell aliases do NOT apply inside scripts — bash only expands them in
# interactive shells. Anything your own `pi` alias adds has to be repeated here
# or pi-local.sh launches without it. --print-only shows what will actually run.
PI_EXTRA_ARGS=

# Keep pi on the current npm release. pi ships often and this stack is only ever
# tested against the latest, so the launcher checks and upgrades in place.
#
# Rate-limited by a stamp file rather than checked every launch — a registry
# round trip per session is latency you would feel, and a local-model stack
# should not need the network to start. The check fails soft in every direction:
# no npm, no network, or a failed install all warn and launch on what is already
# there. Set PI_AUTO_UPDATE=0 to pin whatever version you have.
PI_AUTO_UPDATE=1
PI_UPDATE_INTERVAL_H=24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pi in a container (scripts/pi-container.sh + Dockerfile.pi)
#
# Optional, and off unless PI_CONTAINER_HOME_HOST is set. It gives a pi session
# a home directory of its own, with the browser stack in the same image, and is
# otherwise identical: pi-container.sh delegates to pi-local.sh inside, so the
# flags, the banner and the session are the same ones. docs/container.md.
#
# The container is long-lived and reused rather than created per session, on
# purpose: the browser server and its Chrome live in it and are stateful.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED to use it, and the one thing that cannot be defaulted: the agent's
# home directory AS THE DOCKER DAEMON SEES IT. This shell cannot derive it —
# the daemon resolves bind sources, not the caller — and on Docker Desktop it
# must be docker's form of the Windows path (//c/path/to/pi-home). A WSL-style
# source silently mounts an EMPTY directory rather than failing, which arrives
# as "my home is gone" instead of as a bad path. Same rule as MODELS_DIR.
#
# Machine-specific by nature, so set it in .env.local rather than here.
PI_CONTAINER_HOME_HOST=
# Where that directory appears inside the container. Only change it if you also
# change the Dockerfile — pi-container.sh maps host paths onto it.
PI_CONTAINER_HOME=/home/piuser
# The checkout pi-container.sh delegates to, inside the container. It lives on
# the mount above, so this is a path in the agent's own home.
PI_CONTAINER_REPO=/home/piuser/qwen3.8-forge
PI_CONTAINER_IMAGE=pi-agent:latest
PI_CONTAINER_NAME=instantcoffee-pi
# Chrome. The default 64 MB /dev/shm kills renderer processes under memory
# pressure, which surfaces as pages that half-load and navigate calls that time
# out — i.e. as "the browser is broken" rather than as a container limit.
PI_CONTAINER_SHM=2g
# Seconds to wait for the container to report ready on first create. The first
# start installs pi-mcp-adapter over the network; later starts are seconds.
PI_CONTAINER_READY_TIMEOUT=300
# Extra `docker run` flags, split on whitespace. For a second mount, a memory
# cap, a network — anything this file has no opinion about.
PI_CONTAINER_EXTRA_ARGS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MCP, without MCP (scripts/mcp.sh + skills/mcp-tools)
#
# pi has no MCP support and this stack does not want it — one server can publish
# hundreds of tool schemas into a 64K window before the first message. mcp2cli
# turns an MCP server into a CLI instead, so a schema costs tokens only at the
# moment the model decides to use that one tool.
#
# 1 loads skills/mcp-tools into the session, which is what teaches the model
# that ./scripts/mcp.sh exists. Only the skill's name and description sit in the
# prompt (~60 tokens); pi loads the body on demand. Servers are declared in
# mcp/servers.json.
#
# ON by default. The cost is bounded and known — ~60 tokens of skill description
# per session, and nothing else until the model chooses to call a server — so
# there is no measurement standing between it and being useful. Needs uv on
# PATH; the first call installs the pinned mcp2cli.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP2CLI_ENABLED=1
MCP2CLI_VERSION=3.3.1
# Load-bearing pin, not caution. mcp2cli 3.3.1 reads `Tool.inputSchema`; the MCP
# Python SDK renamed it to `input_schema` in 2.0.0, so an unpinned install
# resolves 2.0.0 and every MCP call dies with AttributeError before reaching the
# server. Verified both ways on 2026-08-12. Raise this only once mcp2cli
# supports SDK 2.x.
MCP_SDK_VERSION=1.29.0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser (scripts/browser.sh + skills/browser)
#
# A real Chrome, driven over CDP by the Zendriver MCP server. Not routed through
# scripts/mcp.sh, for two measured reasons (2026-08-16):
#
#   1. mcp2cli spawns the server per call and its session daemon does not work
#      here, so every call would get a FRESH Chrome — `navigate` would open a
#      page that the next command could never see. browser.sh instead starts the
#      server ONCE over HTTP and every call reaches that one process.
#   2. One mcp2cli invocation costs 11-43 s on this box; one direct tools/call
#      POST costs 24 ms. A browser task is 20+ calls.
#
# 1 loads skills/browser into the session (~120 tokens of name and description;
# pi loads the body only when a task matches). The server itself starts on first
# use and stops with `./scripts/browser.sh down`, so nothing runs until asked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BROWSER_MCP_ENABLED=1
# Checkout of https://github.com/coffeegrind123/Zendriver-MCP-fork. NOT vendored:
# it pulls zendriver plus a Chrome, which has no business inside this repo.
# Empty falls back to /opt/zendriver-mcp, then ~/Zendriver-MCP.
ZENDRIVER_MCP_DIR=/opt/zendriver-mcp
BROWSER_MCP_HOST=127.0.0.1
BROWSER_MCP_PORT=8931
# Chrome needs an X display even when nothing looks at it. The container runs
# Xvfb on :99; on a desktop, leave this empty to inherit the session's DISPLAY.
BROWSER_MCP_DISPLAY=:99
# 1 lets a tool call start the server (and open a browser) by itself. 0 makes
# both explicit, which is what you want if a stray call must never launch Chrome.
BROWSER_MCP_AUTOSTART=1
# Seconds a single tool call may take, as seen by the CLI (scripts/browser.sh).
# Page loads behind a challenge, lighthouse runs and screencast exports are the
# slow ones, and the CLI is driven by a human who can wait.
BROWSER_MCP_TIMEOUT=180

# Seconds a single tool call may take INSIDE THE SERVER, which is a different
# question and has to be answered against the client that is waiting.
#
# The server has always guarded its own calls (ToolBase._register wraps every
# tool in asyncio.wait_for, 120s by default), but pi's adapter gives up at
# requestTimeoutMs = 30000. The server therefore lost the race every time: it
# kept working for up to 120s while the model had already been handed
# "Failed to call tool: Request timed out" plus a dump of the tool's parameters
# — which names nothing about the browser and reads as a bad argument.
#
# Observed 2026-08-17: navigate to a URL whose page.get() never returns, then
# every following call timing out the same way, and the model concluding the
# browser was broken and falling back to curl. The page had in fact loaded.
#
# Sized BELOW the adapter's 30s so the server always answers first, with a real
# sentence ("Tool 'navigate' exceeded its 25s time budget") that says which tool
# and how long. Raise the adapter's requestTimeoutMs too if you raise this.
BROWSER_MCP_TOOL_TIMEOUT=25


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The browser as native pi tools (pi-mcp-adapter)
#
# The hybrid: scripts/browser.sh still OWNS the server process — it outlives a
# pi session and is shared with anything else on the box — while pi reaches it
# through pi-mcp-adapter instead of shelling out to the CLI. The model then calls
# navigate/click/type_text/get_interaction_tree/get_text_content as ordinary
# tools, and the other 93 stay one mcp({ search }) away.
#
# Measured on 2026-08-16, against this stack's own server, from the bytes pi
# actually sent to a stub model:
#   context   720 tokens for the mcp proxy tool, 302 for mcpScript, 1,155 for
#             the five direct tools = 2,178 of a 32K window (6.6%). Loading all
#             98 schemas the normal MCP way would be ~19k (60%).
#   latency   25-417 ms per call, in-process, against 1.7-6.5 s for the CLI
#             (which pays a Python start and a full tools/list every call) and
#             11-60 s for mcp2cli.
#   batching  mcpScript runs a whole flow — navigate, read, click, read — in ONE
#             tool call, which on a local model is worth more than the tokens.
#
# 0 falls back to the CLI path: skills/browser and ./scripts/browser.sh, which
# needs no npm package and stays the answer for a shell, a script, or a human.
#
# The package is NOT vendored — 42 dependencies and 83 MB installed is not
# something this repo can carry the way it carries the /loop fork. pi-local.sh
# checks it and prints the install command rather than installing it silently:
#   pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter@2.26.0
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_ADAPTER_ENABLED=1
# Pinned, and checked at launch. The adapter is a fast-moving package (2.26.0 was
# published the day before this was wired up); a surprise major would change the
# tool surface the model has been taught, mid-session.
MCP_ADAPTER_VERSION=2.26.0
# 1 starts the browser SERVER at launch when the adapter is on (~2 s, ~50 MB, no
# Chrome until a tool asks for one). Without it the model's first browser call
# hits a closed port, and a model that reads that as "the web is not available
# to me" stops trying for the rest of the session. Chrome still only launches on
# demand, and ./scripts/browser.sh down still stops everything.
BROWSER_MCP_AUTOUP=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pi packages
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unattended loop mode: `/loop start <goal>` iterates the agent until you stop
# it. NOT an npm install any more — scripts/pi-local.sh loads the fork in
# vendor/pi-loop-mode by absolute path, so the loop behaves the same in whatever
# directory pi was started in and the fixes travel with this checkout.
#
# If the upstream package is still in pi's user settings from before the fork,
# remove it: `pi uninstall npm:pi-loop-mode`. Two copies register two /loop
# commands that fight over the same session state; pi-local.sh warns about it.
#
# Chosen over pi-agent-loop after testing both: this one persists loop state as
# session entries and rebuilds context locally on overflow, so a loop survives
# compaction, and its repetition/degenerate-output handling is aimed squarely at
# weaker local models. Verified end to end on Qwen3.6-27B — see
# context/design/decisions.md and vendor/pi-loop-mode/FORK.md.
#
# May the MODEL arm a goal check by itself? 0 (unset) = ask, 1 = yes.
#
# `/loop start … --check "CMD"` runs CMD with `bash -lc` once per iteration, for
# the life of the run and across `/loop resume`. That is the one shell channel in
# this stack that emits no `tool_call`, so the Matrix permission relay, rtk-pi's
# gate and the compaction guard's output cap never see it — which is why
# `--check` is refused from Matrix (AD6).
#
# The `loop` TOOL declares the same parameter, and a tool's caller is the model,
# whose instructions can come from a file it read or from a Matrix message the
# same stack calls untrusted input. So by default the operator is TOLD the model
# asked, and ASKED to confirm it when there is anybody to ask; with nobody to ask
# (pi -p, cron) the check is not armed and the loop starts without one, which
# `until_done` handles on the LOOP_DONE marker as it always has.
#
# Set to 1 for an unattended run that genuinely wants the model arming its own
# check. Your own `/loop start --check` in the terminal is unaffected either way:
# that is you choosing the command. See AJ2 in
# context/design/subagents-loop-verifier-authority.md.
LOOP_TOOL_CHECK=0

# Recorded for provenance: the upstream release this fork started from.
PI_LOOP_MODE_VERSION=2.5.4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subagents (vendor/pi-subagents-lite)
#
# Delegate a job to a focused child session that burns its OWN window and hands
# back a bounded summary. pi ships no subagents on purpose ("ask pi to build what
# you want or install a third party pi package"), so this is a vendored fork —
# see vendor/pi-subagents-lite/FORK.md for what was changed and why.
#
# ON by default. It is not free — a registered tool costs its schema on every
# turn whether or not the model ever calls it — but the bill was measured rather
# than feared: 710 chars, ~178 tokens, 0.54% of a 32k window for all three
# tools. For scale, the six prinny tools cost ~1,470 before they were folded
# into one. Set this to 0 to get those 178 tokens back.
#
# Note what a subagent buys HERE. It is not parallelism: llama.cpp runs with
# PARALLEL_SLOTS=1, so concurrent children queue no matter where the queue
# forms, and the fork therefore defaults concurrency to 1 so at most one foreign
# prefix competes with the parent's at a time. What it buys is context
# isolation — the noisy search happens somewhere else.
#
# The cost that actually matters is not the tool schema. Measured: a small subagent leaves the parent's prefix cache alone
# (99.2% hit), but one that actually reads files and grows to ~18k tokens
# evicts it completely, and the parent's next turn re-prefills — 442 ms to
# 2,949 ms on a 2,133-token parent, and a real session carries more than that.
# Delegate work worth a re-prefill; do not delegate a lookup.
SUBAGENTS_ENABLED=1

# What a subagent loads that it could not otherwise reach.
#
# A child does not inherit the parent's -e flags: it discovers its own
# extensions, so everything in .pi/extensions/ (the compaction guard, /stack,
# the browser guard) reaches it and everything in vendor/ does not. Measured on
# a live subagent: 12 tools, one skill, nothing from vendor/.
#
# Unset means the fork's default — vendor/pi-loop-mode and vendor/rtk-pi. loop
# because a bounded goal loop belongs in a window that is not the operator's;
# rtk because a child running bash uncompressed is the session that can least
# afford it. Set this to a comma- or colon-separated list of absolute paths to
# replace that default, or to the empty string for none.
#
# vendor/prinny-channel is denied unconditionally and cannot be added here: a
# subagent is spawned by the model, with a prompt you never see, and it does not
# get to post to a Matrix room. Its two skills are denied with it.
#SUBAGENT_EXTRA_EXTENSIONS=

# Check a subagent's answer against the task before the parent reads it.
#
# ON unless set to 0. Three layers, cheapest first, so most runs pay nothing
# extra: the task is restated into the child's context after each compaction
# (free, and the layer that matters most — it stops the drift instead of
# catching it); an empty answer or a run that hit the turn ceiling is handled
# without a model call; and only a non-empty answer from a clean run is worth
# asking a judge about, because that is the only case where drift is invisible.
#
# The judge is a fresh one-turn agent with no tools that sees ONLY the task and
# the answer. Not the child itself: a model shown its own reasoning ratifies its
# own answer, so the judge is made harder to fool by knowing less. A failed
# verdict continues the CHILD, since that is where the context to fix it is, and
# the fix is then re-judged.
#
# It costs nothing in schema — the verifier agent is hidden from the Agent
# tool's type list, measured — but a judged answer does cost one small model
# call on the same single slot the parent is waiting on. Set to 0 to skip it.
SUBAGENT_VERIFY=1

# How many times a wrong answer may be sent back to the child.
#
# A round is a repair plus the re-check that follows it: two model calls on the
# one llama slot the parent is blocked on, and two more turns in a child window
# that is already the thing most likely to be at fault. So the worst case is
# 1 + 2*ROUNDS calls for a single subagent answer.
#
# The loop also stops early on an empty repair and on a repair identical to the
# answer just rejected — a child that repeats itself has nothing more to give,
# and another round would buy the same verdict at full price.
#
# 0 checks but never repairs (report-only). Values above 3 are clamped: past
# that the honest fix is a narrower task, not a more insistent verifier.
# Unset or unreadable falls back to 1 rather than 0, so a typo cannot silently
# turn the repair off in a way that looks like everything passing.
SUBAGENT_VERIFY_ROUNDS=1

# How long one verification model call may take before it is abandoned, in ms.
#
# There has to be a number, because nothing else can end these calls.
# Verification runs inside the settlement chain, AFTER the child's status has
# gone terminal — and every stop path in the manager keys off "running". So
# while a judge or a repair is in flight the operator's Esc reaches abort() and
# does nothing, the StopAgent tool likewise, and the watchdog drops the record's
# state rather than watching it. The parent's Agent tool call is meanwhile
# blocked until verification returns. A wedged llama-server therefore hung the
# parent with no way out but killing pi — and this stack has seen llama-server
# wedge badly enough that an 8-token completion timed out at 60s while /health
# answered instantly.
#
# 300000 (5 min) is long enough that a slow but working 27B is never cut off and
# short enough that a wedge is a pause, not a hang. A timeout surfaces as the
# verifier's "errored" verdict: the answer still goes out, annotated as
# unchecked. There is no way to spell "no deadline" — 0 clamps to the 10s floor,
# because a disabled deadline is indistinguishable from the bug it prevents.
SUBAGENT_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_MS=300000

# A subagent's own turns in the operator's session transcript. Default on.
#
# One entry per child TURN, marked `subagent <id> <type> · turn N`, bounded at
# 60 entries per delegation and 4,000 characters each. It costs the MODEL
# nothing on any turn — pi's `sessionEntryToContextMessages` returns [] for a
# `type: "custom"` entry, so it persists and renders and is never context — but
# it does cost bytes in the session file, and an unattended /loop that delegates
# every iteration writes a lot of them.
#
# Set to 0 to write nothing. (Twenty-third pass, AN4: this line reached the
# process for the first time when `scripts/pi-local.sh` began forwarding it.)
#SUBAGENT_TRANSCRIPT=0

# The judge's raw prompt and reply, one JSONL line per verifier model call, in
# ~/.pi/agent/subagent-verify.jsonl. Default on.
#
# It is the file that makes a claim about how a verdict was PARSED checkable: a
# reply and the parse beside it is the only thing that can show the parser was
# wrong, and four findings in this series each needed one. Bounded at 2,000
# lines, newest kept, 4,000 characters per field.
#
# Set to 0 to write nothing; SUBAGENT_VERIFY_LOG_FILE moves it. (AN4: neither
# was forwarded before the twenty-third pass.)
#SUBAGENT_VERIFY_LOG=0
#SUBAGENT_VERIFY_LOG_FILE=

# Recorded for provenance: the upstream release this fork started from.
PI_SUBAGENTS_LITE_VERSION=1.11.0

# Tell the model to USE the subagents it already has.
#
# 1 = append prompts/delegate.md to the system prompt; 0 = do not. Inert unless
# SUBAGENTS_ENABLED=1, because a fragment describing a tool that is not
# registered is worse than nothing.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The capability was never the problem — FORK.md's live run
# against this model records it emitting "a well-formed Agent call with a
# self-contained prompt and a sensible description, did not search itself, and
# got a correct answer back", and inferring run_in_background and AgentStatus
# from parameter names alone. It delegates well WHEN ASKED. Nothing in the
# session ever asked. The Agent tool carries no description on purpose (upstream
# deletes it to save tokens; see FORK.md), so the only thing advertising
# delegation was the word "Agent" and an enum listing general-purpose,Explore.
#
# WHY A PROMPT FRAGMENT AND NOT AN AGENT TYPE. A custom type in
# ~/.pi/agent/agents/ would auto-populate that enum for the cost of its name
# alone (registration.ts:33 builds the schema as `types.join(",")`, so the
# frontmatter description never reaches the wire). That is genuinely cheap — but
# `Explore` is ALREADY a built-in, and already in the enum, and its own one-line
# summary is "Explore codebase architecture". A codebase-research type would
# duplicate a built-in. The menu was never missing; the instruction to look at
# it was.
#
# THE FRAGMENT ARGUES BOTH WAYS ON PURPOSE, and that is the part not to edit out
# casually. vram-adjacent measurement in FORK.md: a small child leaves the
# parent's prefix cache at 99.2%, but one that actually reads files and grows to
# ~18k tokens EVICTS it, and the parent's next call goes 442 ms -> 2,949 ms on a
# 2,133-token parent — a floor, not a worst case. So a nudge that simply said
# "delegate more" would make this stack SLOWER by trading cheap reads for
# re-prefills. delegate.md therefore carries the threshold (~five file reads or
# three searches) as well as the encouragement.
#
# ~433 tokens, every turn, against a 98,304-token window. For scale
# prompts/web-untrusted.md is ~460 and the whole three-tool subagent schema is
# ~178.
#
# UNMEASURED. This is a behavioural prompt change adopted ahead of evidence,
# which is the same footing THINK_LANG=zh is on and is called out as such in
# prompts/README.md. What it should move is turns-per-session and how far a task
# gets before the window fills — not correctness. Set to 0 for the control arm.
SUBAGENT_NUDGE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matrix channel (vendor/prinny-channel)
#
# Talk to a pi session — and through it the local model — from any Matrix
# client. A message from an allowlisted sender becomes a turn; the answer is
# forwarded back automatically, which is the point: a 27B model writes its
# answer into the transcript instead of calling a reply tool, and with the
# upstream Claude Code design that answer reached nobody.
#
# OFF by default, and deliberately so. This is the only thing in this stack that
# logs into a remote service and makes the session addressable from the
# internet — that is a decision to take on purpose, not a default to inherit.
# 0 leaves the extension out of the session entirely rather than loading it in a
# dormant state, so there is nothing to misconfigure until you ask for it.
#
# Turning it on:
#   PRINNY_ENABLED=1, then in a pi session:
#     /prinny prepare                                    once, ~1 min
#     /prinny configure https://server @bot:server pass
#     (message the bot from your client, it replies with a code)
#     /prinny pair <code>
#     /prinny policy allowlist                           lock it down
#
# Credentials live in ~/.pi/agent/channels/prinny/.env, mode 600 — NOT here.
# This file is committed; that one is not, and is written by /prinny configure.
#
# One Matrix account per channel. Two bots on one account duplicate every
# delivery and fight over the crypto store. See vendor/prinny-channel/FORK.md.
#
# It is also not free to have loaded, though that argument is much weaker than
# it was. Measured 2026-08-16 off the wire, the six prinny_* tool schemas were
# 1,470 tokens — 4.5% of a 32,768-token window, spent every turn before the
# model read a word. Re-measured 2026-08-17 with the same harness after the six
# were folded into one `prinny` tool dispatching on `action`: the channel's
# whole tool surface is 1,333 chars, ~333 tokens. The inbound wrapper went the
# same way — a message is now `[matrix] <text>` rather than a fourteen-attribute
# <channel> block, 38 chars where it was 249.
#
# So the remaining reason to leave this at 0 is the EXPOSURE, not the cost: it
# logs a bot into a homeserver and makes the session addressable from the
# internet. Turn it on when you are using Matrix, not by default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRINNY_ENABLED=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bash output compression (scripts/rtk.sh + vendor/rtk-pi)
#
# Same arithmetic as the MCP block above, pointed at the other big consumer of a
# 32K window. Bash output is not billed here, it is rented: every byte of pytest
# chatter is a byte that is not the file the model was asked to read. rtk filters
# a command's output before pi ever sees it — `git status` 275 -> 49 bytes,
# `pytest -q` on 43 tests 1312 -> 476, both measured in this repo on 2026-08-16.
#
# 1 loads vendor/rtk-pi into the session. Without the binary on PATH the
# extension warns once and filters nothing, so this is safe to leave on before
# `./scripts/rtk.sh --install` has ever been run.
#
# It filters an ALLOW-LIST, not everything. Upstream's extension hands every bash
# command to rtk; this fork does not, because some rewrites substitute a
# different command — `npm run lint` becomes a bare eslint, `uv run pytest`
# resolves a pytest outside the venv — and because most other filters save
# nothing here anyway. Read vendor/rtk-pi/FORK.md before widening it, and
# re-measure with `./scripts/rtk.sh --check` after changing the pin.
#
# RTK_DISABLED=1 in the environment turns filtering off for one launch without
# editing this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTK_ENABLED=1
# Pinned deliberately. rtk shipped 45 minor versions in seven months and its
# filters are what the allow-list is trusting; an unpinned filter set can change
# what a command reports between two sessions with nothing in the checkout to
# show for it. Raise it, then run ./scripts/rtk.sh --check before committing.
RTK_VERSION=0.45.0
